      "message": "Done",
      "parentUuid": "a3363ca4_a1142984",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 25,
        "startChar": 39,
        "endLine": 25,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "0d1d440444d8b479b94b799f56c7efe44c9e00bf",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "06c5afbe_78a26482",
        "filename": "experimental/graphite/src/BackendTexture.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 49,
      "author": {
        "id": 5036
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-11-29T15:28:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This awkwardness all comes from the fact that we store an sk_cfp of the mtl texture. If we didn\u0027t have the BackendTexture take a ref then this assignment would be trivial.\n\nAn alternative here is that we have BackendTexture take an mtl::Handle (id\u003cMTLTexture), but state that BackendTexture will call Retain and Release on the object. Then inside here we can be explicit with when we need to Release the previous texture and not have the ugliness of calling fMtlTexture.release() on an uninitialized sk_cfp object.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 44,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 49,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "0d1d440444d8b479b94b799f56c7efe44c9e00bf",
      "serverId": "5086850b-e599-37d2-8ec8-ff16aeccbf02"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b3a5b54a_d690b5c3",
        "filename": "experimental/graphite/src/BackendTexture.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 49,
      "author": {
        "id": 5065
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-11-29T16:26:42Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "We could have BackendTexture take a mtl::Handle, but then ref it once we store it in the mtl::Texture.",
      "parentUuid": "06c5afbe_78a26482",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 44,
        "startChar": 11,
        "endLine": 49,
        "endChar": 13
      },
